namespace ArrayList
{
    public class ArrayList
    {
        public int AddWithAssert(Object value)
        {
            // The original value of x, when the method is entered
            int oldX = Count;

            // Call the method we want to test
            int returnValue = Add(value);

            // Make the assert statement
            // We assume that Count is the variable which is affected by the method
            Debug.Assert(oldX + 1 == returnValue, "Value of Count does not increase by 1 in the method Add");

            // Return value which the function actually returns
            return returnValue;
        }
        
        public int Add(Object value)
        {
            // ...
            return _size++;
        }
        
        public int Count
        {
            get { return _size; }
        }
        
        // ...

        // Rest of the class
    }
}
```
This code reads through the C# source code and if it encounters a method where the comments begin with "@@@", it generates an assertion statement. It considers that the variables which can have a value before entering the function is the return value (return variable) and the variables which are affected by the function are equal to their old state before entering the function (old variables). 

The resultant assertion may look like assert(countBefore +1 = countAfter).  This would fail if the method did not correctly increment the variable being modified by 1. Note: The solution assumes that the function increments x by 1 and return the result to be x. It could fail for a method which does not increment or return the correct value. This solution is a generic solution and depends heavily on the implementation details which could be different in another scenario.--------------------Here is the output corresponding to the assertions mentioned in the comments. The RET variable is the return value of the function and OLD(variable_name) is the value of the variable before the function call. Keep in mind that I've only converted the assert statements in the given C# code. There are other conditions like checking for elements in array, comparing the capacity, and other related assertions that also mentioned in the comments. In general, this could vary depending upon the C# code:

